  "response": "## Don't start from `prettyDueDate`\n\n`crimes context src/date.ts` flags one locale-naive renderer — **`prettyDueDate`** (`evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app/src/date.ts:45-47`):\n\n```ts\nexport function prettyDueDate(due: Date): string {\n  return due.toLocaleDateString();\n}\n```\n\n**Method it calls:** `toLocaleDateString()` — invoked with no locale argument.\n\n**Finding evidence, verbatim** (`crime_00029`, `locale_drift` / \"Host-Locale Drift\", severity `low`, confidence `0.85`):\n\n> - `due.toLocaleDateString() @L46`\n> - `lines: 46`\n> - ``pass an explicit locale (e.g. `'en-US'`) or use Intl.DateTimeFormat``\n\nSummary line, also verbatim:\n\n> 1 `toLocale*()` call with no locale argument. Output varies by the runtime's default locale, not by anything the code controls.\n\nCopying it into the billing UI means the rendered due date depends on the host's default locale — `12/25/2026` on a US server, `25/12/2026` on an EU one, with no code-level control. Pass an explicit BCP-47 locale, or build one `Intl.DateTimeFormat` instance at the display boundary and reuse it.\n\nTwo adjacent traps in the same file if you go looking for a different starting point: `dateLabel` (`L39-42`) is charged twice — `mixed_utc_local_methods` (`\"d\" uses getUTCFullYear() @L9 and getMonth() @L41`) and `date_string_concat` — and `tomorrowMs` (`L51-53`) carries `dst_naive_arithmetic` (`+ 86400000 (day) @L52`). If the due-date feature needs \"tomorrow,\" don't take that one either.",
